{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00591 - MONDO_5480",
  "verbalization": "Fluocinolone Acetonide is implicated in the pathogenesis of Contact Dermatitis through a chain of indications that link it to Chronic Cutaneous Lupus Erythematosus (CCLS), a condition that is itself associated with multiple corticosteroids. The evidence suggests that Fluocinolone Acetonide may act as a precursor in the treatment of CCLS, with its use potentially leading to the development of Contact Dermatitis. This is supported by the shared indication of CCLS with desoximetasone and flumethasone, both of which are corticosteroids that are known to induce Contact Dermatitis in susceptible individuals. The presence of a direct indication from Fluocinolone Acetonide to CCLS, and from CCLS to Contact Dermatitis, indicates a possible mechanism involving corticosteroid-induced dermatitis. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of corticosteroids in the development of Contact Dermatitis, particularly in the context of autoimmune conditions like CCLS. The evidence suggests a dependency on corticosteroids, which may be a key factor in the pathophysiology of Contact Dermatitis, though further research is needed to fully elucidate the exact mechanism.",
